    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d395">

                <pb facs="0536" n="524" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg524"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 P.W./5–2846: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-28T19:00:00+03:00">May 28, 1946—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 28—6:21 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1678. I saw Molotov this afternoon and raised the question of the Pauley Mission.
                    He informed me that Pauley and his party had <pb facs="0537" n="525"
                        xml:id="pg525"/> already arrived in Northern Korea,<anchor n="52"
                        xml:id="d395-f1"/> but he declined to agree officially to any modification
                    of the original time limit. I gathered from his remarks that the Foreign Office
                    attitude is a reflection of irritation in the Soviet Army which obviously has
                    been objecting to the Pauley Mission, and that the original concession was about
                    as far as Foreign Office could go. I am of the opinion that Mr. Pauley will be
                    given his minimum of 5 days for visiting plants and economic facilities.</p>
                <p>Department please repeat to Tokyo as 47 and Seoul.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d395-f1" n="52" xml:id="d395-f1-content">
                <p>The Department in its telegram 984, May 28, 8 p.m., to Moscow, quoted Mr. Pauley
                    as reporting his party of 26 (10 principals, 2 aides, 5 interpreters, 3
                    secretaries, 1 train chief, 1 surgeon, and 4 drivers) expected to leave May 29
                    at 7 a.m. for north Korea (740.00119 P.W./5–2846).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
